Steam store page copy. Paste each block into the field named above it.

Steam takes BBCode, not HTML, so this is not docs/itch/description.html with the
tags swapped -- and images CANNOT be hotlinked the way the itch page pulls them
off jsdelivr. Upload each screenshot under Asset Upload -> extras, then refer to
it as {STEAM_APP_IMAGE}/extras/<name>.png. An external [img] URL is stripped and
you find out after publishing.

================================================================================
SHORT DESCRIPTION            (Basic Info -> Short Description, 300 char maximum)
================================================================================

A free, source-available grand strategy game. Run a country's industry, armies,
research and politics across 1641 provinces and six historical scenarios, from
1914 to the modern day. Build your own world in the map editor, play multiplayer
with no port forwarding, and mod it in thirteen languages.

================================================================================
ABOUT THIS GAME                                  (Basic Info -> About This Game)
================================================================================

[h2]Run a country[/h2]

OpenDoctrines is a grand strategy game about the whole of a state: its industry,
its armies, its research, its politics and its neighbours. It is free, it is
source-available, and there is nothing to buy inside it.

Provinces have population, industry, fortification, resources and an ethnic
composition. Countries have a treasury, a research programme, a political
compass, claims on their neighbours and opinions about each other.

Industry is bounded by the ground it stands on. Each province states the level
it can support, set by its population, how densely that population lives, its
land area and its mineral wealth. Building an industrial power means holding
places worth industrialising, not buying the same factory everywhere.

[img]{STEAM_APP_IMAGE}/extras/province.png[/img]

[h2]Six scenarios on a 1641-province world[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Modern Day[/b] (2000) — 185 countries. Population from World Bank totals, deposits from USGS surveys.
[*][b]The Powder Keg[/b] (1914) — five empires and a continent of alliances waiting on one funeral.
[*][b]The Last Spring[/b] (1918) — Germany has won in the east and is losing in the west.
[*][b]The Gathering Storm[/b] (1939) — Europe on the morning the Wehrmacht crossed the Polish border.
[*][b]Year Zero[/b] (1945) — the war is over and nothing has been settled.
[*][b]The Missile Crisis[/b] (1962) — two blocs, one ocean between them, and missiles in Cuba.
[/list]

Doctrines sit on a left/right and authoritarian/libertarian compass, with
implementation times, an ethnic policy, and the unrest that follows a bad one.
Rebellions spawn from that unrest and hold real territory. Wars pull in guarantee
chains. Ceasefires are negotiated over provinces, money and dropped claims.

[img]{STEAM_APP_IMAGE}/extras/world-map.png[/img]

[h2]Every turn is recorded[/h2]

A finished game replays as a timelapse and exports as a GIF from inside the
game — the same history rendered as politics, as population, or as troop
concentration.

[h2]Build your own world[/h2]

Draw land and sea, cut provinces, place countries, populations, resources and
claims, then export a map anyone else can load. There is a procedural generator
too, for when you want [i]a[/i] world rather than a specific one.

[img]{STEAM_APP_IMAGE}/extras/map-editor.png[/img]

[h2]Multiplayer that needs no port forwarding[/h2]

Players dial out over an encrypted connection, so joining needs no router
configuration. Hosting is a listening socket on the host's machine, and the game
can open a tunnel to it for you.

The host is authoritative: state only ever flows server to client, orders are
validated server-side and re-attributed to the authenticated player's country,
and a client never computes a turn. [b]A player who disconnects keeps their seat
and their country[/b] — the turn resolves without them, and they get that same
country back when they return.

[h2]Mods, in thirteen languages[/h2]

Mods are WebAssembly, so a mod is the same file on every platform, and it runs in
a sandbox with a capability grant you can see and revoke. The SDK has bindings
for C, C++, Rust, Zig, Go, Java, Kotlin, JavaScript, TypeScript, AssemblyScript,
Lua, Python and hand-written WAT — every one builds the same example, and the
test suite checks they all render byte-identical output.

[img]{STEAM_APP_IMAGE}/extras/mods.png[/img]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]What is not finished[/h2]

Being honest about an alpha is cheaper than disappointing people later.

[list]
[*][b]The tutorial is young.[/b] There is one, with a map built for it and an advisor who talks you through a first game, and the unrest lesson is translated into all twenty languages. What it has not had is somebody who has never played a grand strategy game watched over the shoulder, which is the only test that counts.
[*][b]The AI plays a whole game and will not outplay a good player.[/b] It is a neural network trained by self-play against a league of its own frozen past versions. It reliably beats random play and is currently about level with the hand-written strategy it is measured against — a rung it has reached and not yet passed. The difficulty setting changes how much noise it adds to its own choices rather than how well it plays.
[*][b]The dedicated server is young.[/b] It is smoke-tested on every platform before release but has not been run in anger by anybody.
[*][b]Play-by-post turns are built but not yet played.[/b] The whole path exists and is unit-tested; what has not happened is a real campaign across several days on two machines.
[/list]

[h2]No macOS build on Steam[/h2]

Steam requires macOS builds to be signed and notarized, and this project pays for
neither. Mac players are not left out — the macOS builds are free on
[url=https://pr1nted.itch.io/open-doctrines]itch.io[/url] and
[url=https://github.com/Pr1nted/Open-Doctrines/releases]GitHub[/url], along with
an Android APK and a browser version that needs no download at all.

[h2]Free, and source-available[/h2]

Free to play, modify and share non-commercially, under the OpenDoctrines
Non-Commercial License. The mods, maps, saves and videos you make are yours. The
code is on [url=https://github.com/Pr1nted/Open-Doctrines]GitHub[/url]; the
people are on [url=https://discord.gg/wqS65jzVv5]Discord[/url].

