================================================================================
EARLY ACCESS QUESTIONNAIRE                    (recommended -- see the note below)
================================================================================

Ship this as Early Access. The game IS an alpha, and Steam has a formal label
that says so. Without it, "no tutorial" and "the AI is beatable" arrive as
negative reviews from people who expected a finished game; with it, they arrive
as feedback. A free game can be Early Access.

Why Early Access?
  "The game is playable from end to end and is not finished. The tutorial is
  young, the AI is still being trained, and two systems -- the dedicated
  server and play-by-post turns -- are built and tested but have never been
  used in anger by a real group. We would rather say that on the label than in
  an apology later."

How long will this be in Early Access?
  "Until the tutorial has been proven on first-time players, the AI stops
  improving measurably between builds,
  and a play-by-post campaign has been run start to finish by people who are
  not the developer. We are not naming a date we cannot keep."

How is the full version planned to differ?
  "A tutorial, a stronger AI, and the two young systems above proven rather than
  merely tested. Everything already in the game stays in it. Nothing is being
  held back for a paid version, because there is no paid version."

What is the current state?
  "Six scenarios on a 1641-province world map, a full economic, research,
  political and military model, rebellion and diplomacy, a map editor with a
  procedural generator, host-authoritative multiplayer, and a WebAssembly mod
  SDK with bindings for thirteen languages. The 'What is not finished' section
  of the store page lists every gap we know about."

Will the game be priced differently during and after Early Access?
  "No. The game is free now and will be free afterwards."

How are you involving the community?
  "Bug reports and suggestions are sent from inside the game, and the code and
  issue tracker are public on GitHub. Discussion, and most of the arguing about
  what to build next, happens on Discord."
